WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:02.340
What once was old is new again.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.820
Once more an official LEGO set for an upcoming
Marvel flick— in this case, the long-awaited

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.640
Avengers: Endgame — may have revealed a
major spoiler.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.840
Namely, the return of the Chitauri, the invading
alien race from the first Avengers movie.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:21.380
Over the years, plot points such as the destruction
of Tony Stark's home in Iron Man 3, the presence

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:26.009
of the Hulkbuster armor in Avengers: Age of
Ultron, Scott Lang "going big" during the

00:00:26.009 --> 00:00:31.269
airport brawl in Captain America: Civil War,
and more have been spoiled for fans by LEGO

00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:34.540
sets released in advance of their respective
films' debuts.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.900
One leaked official set for Endgame appears
to be carrying on this tradition with the

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.480
inclusion of minifigs which really can't be
anything other than Chitauri warriors, who

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.960
laid waste to New York in the climactic battle
in the first Avengers film.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.320
The attack was shown in that film's post-credits
sequence to have been orchestrated by Thanos,

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:56.520
but it was headed up by Loki, who was under
the malicious influence of the Infinity Stone

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.520
housed in his scepter.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.600
Bolstering this speculation are set photos
from Endgame that appeared late last year,

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.300
showing our heroes revisiting the Battle of
New York, presumably as part of their master

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.560
plan to reverse the extinction of half of
all life in the universe brought about by

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.240
Thanos and his finger snap at the end of Infinity
War.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.620
The presence of Chitauri warriors in the LEGO
set suggests that the Avengers won't simply

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:23.840
be picking through the rubble for clues, but
may actually have to relive the battle in

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.600
one form or another — possibly even purposefully
altering its outcome, in order to create conditions

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.440
favorable to bringing about the one in 14
million-plus possibilities that results in

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.520
the heroes' eventual victory over Thanos,
as outlined by Doctor Strange.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:44.030
The LEGO set also features a new Hulkbuster-like
suit for War Machine, so it's likely that

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.500
this do-over won't just be a tired rehash.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:50.980
So the likelihood that our heroes will have
to engage in a bit of light time travel in

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.440
order to reverse Thanos' victory is all but
certain at this point.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.240
But it might not be actual time travel: remember
Tony Stark's technology from Captain America:

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:04.950
Civil War which allowed users to holographically
reconstruct incidents from their past using

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:05.820
their memories?

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:09.800
If that's what we're seeing, the return to
the Battle of New York would constitute some

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:14.690
kind of fact-finding mission, perhaps a search
for some weakness of Thanos that was overlooked

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:16.680
the first time around in all the carnage.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:21.260
But we've gotten a few significant clues in
the intervening months that this is likely

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:22.300
not the case.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.140
First, there's the post-credits scene of Ant-Man
and the Wasp, in which Scott wound up trapped

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:31.560
in the Quantum Realm when those tasked with
pulling him back out were all dusted before

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.260
they could do so.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.660
Before entering the Quantum Realm, Scott was
sternly warned …

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.700
"Don't get sucked into a time vortex.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.980
We won't be able to save you."

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:44.800
The first teaser trailer for Endgame revealed
that Scott was indeed able to extract himself

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:45.740
somehow.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.660
Will the venerable Ant-Man's experience with
the Quantum Realm come into play in the Avengers'

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:50.200
plan?

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.840
Will Scott, as he is wont to do, take exactly
the action he was warned not to, employing

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.220
some timey-wimey trickery to help the team
revisit the Battle of New York?

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.200
It certainly seems possible.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.320
On the other hand, we also know that Brie
Larson's Carol Danvers, AKA Captain Marvel

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:10.990
— set to appear in her own '90s-set feature
next month — will play a major role in the

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:15.680
events of Endgame, as teased by the Infinity
War post-credits sequence in which Samuel

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.900
L. Jackson's Nick Fury puts out a distress
call to the good Captain before he, too, crumbles

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:21.440
to dust.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.020
In a recent interview with TotalFilm, Jackson
made a comment about Danvers' power set that

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.360
could be seen as particularly revealing.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:30.680
Jackson said,

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.620
"[Captain Marvel] can time travel, so maybe
she can get ahead or behind or whatever, and

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:36.820
figure out what all that is.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.120
The fact I have the pager [used to put out
the distress call] 20 years later — it gets

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.120
addressed in an interesting sort of way."

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:50.060
Now, this could be seen as a sort of off-the-cuff
comment, as Captain Marvel's time travel abilities

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:51.420
had never been brought up prior.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:56.260
But it can also be assumed that Jackson knows
plenty about the events of Endgame that we

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.940
don't, and he just might have let a major
plot point slip.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:04.260
However it happens, the good folks at LEGO
have clued us in to the fact that Earth's

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.960
Mightiest Heroes will indeed have to relive
their greatest victory, one way or another.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.880
We'll see what it all means when Avengers:
Endgame hits theaters on April 26th, 2019.

